-- title:
--   Lemma 4.2, (4.1) (corrected $U$: Corollary 3.5 integrand $\varphi(s-1/2)$): $\mathbb E_{x,y,z}(Y_\tau-Z_\tau-X_\tau^2)\le U(x,y,z)$ for bounded $\tau$
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge3$, let $U$ be the explicit candidate of Section 3, and let $(x,y,z)\in\mathcal D$. For every bounded stopping time $\tau$ of the natural filtration of the spider started from $(x,y,z)$, the random variable $Y_\tau-Z_\tau-X_\tau^2$ is $P_{x,y,z}$-integrable and
--   $$\mathbb E_{x,y,z}\big(Y_\tau-Z_\tau-X_\tau^2\big)\le U(x,y,z).$$
--
--   At $(0,0,0)$ this is the inequality $\mathbb E D_\tau\le U(0,0,0)+\mathbb E\tau$ for bounded stopping times of the spider, which drives the upper bound in Theorem 1.1.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The stopping times are those of the filtration generated by the position of the general-start spider. It contains the filtration of $(X,Y,Z)$, and at the origin it is the filtration of the spider $S$ itself.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: any bounded stopping time τ (of the process (X, Y, Z)) → any bounded stopping time of the filtration generated by the position of the general-start spider; reason: that filtration contains the natural filtration of (X, Y, Z), so the statement specializes to the source's; at (0,0,0) it covers the stopping times of S used in the proof of Theorem 1.1
--   - reading: "for all (x, y, z)" → for all (x, y, z) in the state space D; reason: D is the state space of p.11 on which U is constructed and the process lives
--   - encoding: E_{x,y,z}(Y_τ − Z_τ − X_τ²) → the integrand is asserted integrable and its Bochner integral bounded; reason: excludes the junk value 0 of a non-integrable integral
--   - correction: U built with the printed Corollary 3.5 (integrand denominator (n − 1)s − φ(y − 1/2)) → the candidate U uses the corrected denominator (n − 1)s − φ(s − 1/2) on {y ≥ 1/2, z > φ(y − 1/2)} and in U(1/2, 1/2, s); reason: the printed Corollary 3.5 contradicts Lemma 3.4 and continuity of U across y = 1/2 (n = 3, (x, z) = (−0.05, −0.1): 0.94325 from (3.12) vs 0.97892 printed); see Corollary 3.5
--   - encoding: the Markov family (P_{x,y,z}) of p.11 → (X, Y, Z) is constructed from (x, y, z) on the same noise (B, θ): radius ||x| + B_t|, a fixed ray (0 if x ≥ 0, 1 if x < 0) until the first zero, initial ribs y on ray 0 and −z on ray 1; P_{x,y,z} is the law of this construction; reason: a concrete realization of the paper's family of initial distributions
--   - reading: P_{x,y,z}((X_0, Y_0, Z_0) = (x, y, z)) = 1 for every (x, y, z) ∈ D → the start (−y, y, −y), y > 0, is the identified point (y, y, −y) (a ray becomes the longest the moment its rib reaches Y); reason: Remark 3.7: "the points (−y, y, −y) and (y, y, −y) in the state space correspond to the same value of U"; the jump rule of p.11
--   - encoding: B is a Brownian motion (a.s. continuous paths, B_0 = 0 a.s.) → every path of B is continuous and starts at 0, and every B_t is measurable; reason: the paper's Brownian motion (Revuz–Yor) is the continuous version; every Brownian motion has such a version
--   - encoding: rays R_k, k = 1, …, n, and complex signs uniform on {e^{2πik/n}} → rays indexed from 0 (Fin n, resp. ℕ); index k is the paper's ray k + 1; the sign θ_m is recorded by its ray index; reason: relabelling; same instances
--   - encoding: the countable set of excursions "can be ordered by the set of natural numbers" (order unspecified) → excursion number = least k such that the k-th enumerated nonnegative rational lies in the excursion; reason: one concrete injective, path-measurable numbering; the law of the spider does not depend on the numbering
--   - reading: U of Section 3 (from Step 7 on, the strategy value) → the explicit function glued from (3.8), Corollary 3.5 and (3.12)–(3.13), with φ^{−1} the two-branch f: f(z) = z + 1/2 for z ≤ −1/2, and the unique s ≥ 0 with φ(s) = z for z > −1/2; reason: Section 4 treats "the function U constructed in the previous section" and Remark 3.7 compares lines of (3.8); Corollaries 3.3, 3.5 and 3.6 identify that function with the strategy value
--   - encoding: bounded stopping time → τ : Ω → [0, ∞] with τ ≤ T everywhere for some T and {τ ≤ t} in the filtration for every t; reason: Mathlib's {τ ≤ t} convention; such a τ is finite everywhere
-- source:
--   Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, On the Diameter of the Stopped Spider Process, arXiv:2105.15202v2, Lemma 4.2, eq. (4.1), p.18

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SpiderDiameter_Noise
import Definitions.Def_SpiderDiameter_Candidate
import Definitions.Def_SpiderDiameter_GeneralProcess

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace SpiderDiameter

/-- **Lemma 4.2 (verification), inequality (4.1)** (Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski,
arXiv:2105.15202v2, Lemma 4.2, p.18): "For any (x, y, z) and any bounded stopping time τ we have
E_{x,y,z}(Y_τ − Z_τ − X_τ²) ≤ U(x, y, z)", with `U` the explicit candidate of §3.

For every `(x, y, z) ∈ 𝒟` and every bounded stopping time `τ` of the natural filtration of the
spider started from `(x, y, z)`: `Y_τ − Z_τ − X_τ²` is integrable under `P_{x,y,z}` and its
expectation is at most `U(x, y, z)`.

**Formalization Note.** The stopping times are those of the filtration generated by the position
of the general-start spider, which contains the filtration of `(X, Y, Z)`; at `(0, 0, 0)` these
are exactly the stopping times of the spider `S` of §1. "for any (x, y, z)" ranges over `𝒟`. -/
theorem lemma_4_2 (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (N : SpiderNoise n P) (x y z : ℝ) (hD : (x, y, z) ∈ stateSpace)
    (τ : Ω → ℝ≥0∞) (hτ : IsGenStoppingTime N x τ) (hbdd : ∃ T : ℝ≥0, ∀ ω, τ ω ≤ T) :
    Integrable (fun ω => atTime (Yg N x y z) τ ω - atTime (Zg N x y z) τ ω
      - atTime (Xg N x y z) τ ω ^ 2) P ∧
    ∫ ω, (atTime (Yg N x y z) τ ω - atTime (Zg N x y z) τ ω - atTime (Xg N x y z) τ ω ^ 2) ∂P
      ≤ candidate n x y z := by sorry

end SpiderDiameter
